WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.617 --> 00:00:12.488
This is "Disney on Ice."

00:00:13.430 --> 00:00:15.245
But as you can see,

00:00:15.245 --> 00:00:18.385
there's much more to
the show than skating.

00:00:19.520 --> 00:00:21.880
Bryan Santiago: One, two, three and four.

00:00:21.880 --> 00:00:23.960
Three, turn, five, six,
crossover, seven, eight.

00:00:23.960 --> 00:00:25.070
Narrator: We got to meet some of

00:00:25.070 --> 00:00:27.800
the show's principal performers.

00:00:27.800 --> 00:00:30.713
Like Nelson Sanchez Leemet,
who plays the role of Woody.

00:00:31.890 --> 00:00:35.070
Maria Starr, who plays Elastigirl.

00:00:35.070 --> 00:00:37.400
And Nathan Miller and Sophia Adams,

00:00:37.400 --> 00:00:40.267
who star as Aladdin and Princess Jasmine.

00:00:43.670 --> 00:00:45.980
We went behind the scenes.

00:00:45.980 --> 00:00:47.246
Nelson Sanchez Leemet: I
feel like I'm a gymnast,

00:00:47.246 --> 00:00:48.604
not gonna lie.

00:00:48.604 --> 00:00:51.327
Narrator: To find out how
these athletic performers

00:00:51.327 --> 00:00:52.960
push themselves to limits

00:00:52.960 --> 00:00:55.610
they didn't even know were possible.

00:00:55.610 --> 00:00:57.650
Maria Starr: Honestly, six
weeks ago, I don't think

00:00:57.650 --> 00:00:59.043
that I could do these,

00:00:59.043 --> 00:01:01.053
and now I could do them and talk to you.

00:01:02.930 --> 00:01:04.370
Narrator: This is what it takes

00:01:04.370 --> 00:01:06.870
to perform in "Disney on Ice."

00:01:13.590 --> 00:01:15.370
We caught up with the cast and crew

00:01:15.370 --> 00:01:18.680
of "Disney on Ice Presents:
Road Trip Adventures,"

00:01:18.680 --> 00:01:21.570
the brand's newest show, just days before

00:01:21.570 --> 00:01:23.500
the company set out on a two-year,

00:01:23.500 --> 00:01:26.680
67-city tour of North America.

00:01:26.680 --> 00:01:27.898
Cindy Stewart: Take a break.

00:01:27.898 --> 00:01:30.148
[clapping]

00:01:31.576 --> 00:01:33.410
Narrator: The show is created here,

00:01:33.410 --> 00:01:36.720
at Feld Entertainment
Studios in Ellenton, Florida,

00:01:36.720 --> 00:01:39.023
about 40 miles south of Tampa.

00:01:40.010 --> 00:01:43.410
Inside the 580,000-square-foot facility,

00:01:43.410 --> 00:01:45.930
the cast and crew have
spent roughly a year

00:01:45.930 --> 00:01:47.080
putting the show together,

00:01:47.080 --> 00:01:49.710
rehearsing and training six days a week,

00:01:49.710 --> 00:01:52.280
for eight hours a day, in a full-size rink

00:01:52.280 --> 00:01:56.560
complete with lights, props,
and high-end LED screens.

00:01:56.560 --> 00:01:58.170
Cindy: We're in our final rehearsals

00:01:58.170 --> 00:02:00.640
before our first preview show.

00:02:00.640 --> 00:02:02.570
And we're just tying up loose ends

00:02:02.570 --> 00:02:04.183
and making last-minute changes.

00:02:05.690 --> 00:02:08.030
Narrator: On the day of our
visit, friends and family

00:02:08.030 --> 00:02:10.900
of Feld Entertainment
employees filled the arena

00:02:10.900 --> 00:02:13.240
to witness a special preview performance

00:02:13.240 --> 00:02:14.798
of "Road Trip Adventures."

00:02:16.770 --> 00:02:19.260
Along with familiar faces like Mickey,

00:02:19.260 --> 00:02:22.030
Minnie, Donald, and Goofy, the new show

00:02:22.030 --> 00:02:24.530
features segments from
recent Disney movies,

00:02:24.530 --> 00:02:29.530
like "Aladdin," "Toy Story 4,"
and "Mary Poppins Returns."

00:02:29.820 --> 00:02:31.440
Nicole Feld: We partner
with the Disney Company

00:02:31.440 --> 00:02:33.900
to understand what's in
their content pipeline.

00:02:33.900 --> 00:02:36.250
What's new and different,
what's coming out.

00:02:36.250 --> 00:02:39.290
And from there we pick out the moments

00:02:39.290 --> 00:02:40.560
that we feel like are gonna

00:02:40.560 --> 00:02:43.135
resonate the most with audiences.

00:02:45.546 --> 00:02:47.620
Producer: You use these
skates for a long time, or?

00:02:47.620 --> 00:02:51.320
Maria: I never have them
more than 11 months.

00:02:51.320 --> 00:02:53.420
It's, like, always 10 or 11 months.

00:02:53.420 --> 00:02:56.210
Bryan: All right, so, "Trip
a Little Light Fantastic."

00:02:56.210 --> 00:02:57.650
The number overall is
looking really great,

00:02:57.650 --> 00:02:59.900
but there's three sections I
really wanna clean up today.

00:02:59.900 --> 00:03:02.010
Five, six, seven, eight.

00:03:02.010 --> 00:03:06.310
One, two, three, four,
five, six, seven, eight.

00:03:06.310 --> 00:03:07.310
One and two.

00:03:07.310 --> 00:03:08.900
Narrator: The "Road Trip Adventures" cast

00:03:08.900 --> 00:03:11.230
features 53 performers.

00:03:11.230 --> 00:03:12.063
Bryan: Much better.

00:03:12.063 --> 00:03:14.250
Narrator: Feld Entertainment
wouldn't share details

00:03:14.250 --> 00:03:16.326
about their salaries but did say

00:03:16.326 --> 00:03:18.660
that they are provided with healthcare.

00:03:18.660 --> 00:03:21.350
Judy Thomas: Most of the skaters
here have grown up skating,

00:03:21.350 --> 00:03:24.240
from young children through competitions.

00:03:24.240 --> 00:03:27.320
And we really do recruit and go out

00:03:27.320 --> 00:03:29.610
and really search the
talent through competitions

00:03:29.610 --> 00:03:32.120
as they're coming up through
the different levels.

00:03:32.120 --> 00:03:35.150
Bryan: One, two, three, and four. Good.

00:03:35.150 --> 00:03:37.410
So, Nelson, just make sure that you're

00:03:37.410 --> 00:03:40.500
heel together, lean back, lean forward.

00:03:40.500 --> 00:03:42.690
Nelson: I was born in the
Dominican Republic, so,

00:03:42.690 --> 00:03:45.320
no winter, no snow, no big rinks.

00:03:45.320 --> 00:03:46.920
Never even thought about figure skating

00:03:46.920 --> 00:03:49.860
until I was 10 when I moved to Canada.

00:03:49.860 --> 00:03:51.970
I didn't really like the
competitive side of skating,

00:03:51.970 --> 00:03:54.010
I was more performing
and letting the audience

00:03:54.010 --> 00:03:55.500
enjoy the performance.

00:03:55.500 --> 00:03:57.320
So when it got too competitive, I kinda

00:03:57.320 --> 00:03:59.620
took a step back and I
looked at "Disney on Ice."

00:03:59.620 --> 00:04:02.480
So I was like, I have to
do that, it's my turn now.

00:04:02.480 --> 00:04:03.480
And here I am today.

00:04:04.940 --> 00:04:06.410
Maria: I skated competitively

00:04:06.410 --> 00:04:09.240
and did that until I
graduated high school.

00:04:09.240 --> 00:04:12.260
And then I just applied
for "Disney on Ice,"

00:04:12.260 --> 00:04:15.210
sent in a skating résumé and,
like, video of my skating.

00:04:15.210 --> 00:04:16.290
And I got offered a job.

00:04:16.290 --> 00:04:18.422
And I was here a month later.

00:04:18.422 --> 00:04:21.000
And I've been on the road,
this'll be my 10th year.

00:04:21.000 --> 00:04:21.866
Nathan Miller: I've actually been skating

00:04:21.866 --> 00:04:22.991
for about 25 years now.

00:04:22.991 --> 00:04:25.640
When I was a kid, I
lived in Oklahoma City,

00:04:25.640 --> 00:04:27.668
and "Disney on Ice" always
came through at the state fair.

00:04:27.668 --> 00:04:29.470
And that was, like, a big
highlight for me as a child.

00:04:29.470 --> 00:04:31.052
And I always as a kid wanted to skate

00:04:31.052 --> 00:04:32.970
alongside Goofy, and, I mean, here I am,

00:04:32.970 --> 00:04:35.095
now eight years, and I still get to do it.

00:04:37.160 --> 00:04:38.451
Oh, it was everything I hoped it would be.

00:04:38.451 --> 00:04:39.284
It was so much fun.

00:04:40.170 --> 00:04:42.980
Sophia Adams: I was just a
competitive skater for the US.

00:04:42.980 --> 00:04:44.760
I've been training for my whole life.

00:04:44.760 --> 00:04:47.580
I've been to Nationals,
I've done the whole thing.

00:04:47.580 --> 00:04:49.870
But I really wanted to travel,

00:04:49.870 --> 00:04:51.870
I really wanted to earn a living

00:04:51.870 --> 00:04:54.230
off of what I do as a career.

00:04:54.230 --> 00:04:56.340
I've been with the company
for about five years.

00:04:56.340 --> 00:04:58.140
I've traveled around the world,

00:04:58.140 --> 00:04:59.840
almost on every tour possible.

00:04:59.840 --> 00:05:01.830
But what is also extremely great about

00:05:01.830 --> 00:05:04.755
this particular show is
I actually get to fly.

00:05:12.140 --> 00:05:14.890
Narrator: While the show
is called "Disney on Ice,"

00:05:14.890 --> 00:05:18.463
some performers spend a bulk
of their time in the air.

00:05:21.770 --> 00:05:23.900
In addition to skating, the show features

00:05:23.900 --> 00:05:26.163
complex aerial choreography.

00:05:30.340 --> 00:05:32.130
Nathan: Do the arabesque
death spiral release,

00:05:32.130 --> 00:05:33.440
and then we'll stop here.

00:05:33.440 --> 00:05:36.528
Narrator: This requires
collaboration between the performers

00:05:36.528 --> 00:05:39.212
and crew members in charge
of operating the cables

00:05:39.212 --> 00:05:41.095
connected to their harnesses.

00:05:41.095 --> 00:05:42.680
Danica O'Neill: A lot of
the people that we have

00:05:42.680 --> 00:05:45.150
have never really flown
before or done any aerial work

00:05:45.150 --> 00:05:47.000
because they are naturally skaters.

00:05:47.000 --> 00:05:48.410
So where they've started

00:05:48.410 --> 00:05:50.230
and where they are now has grown so much.

00:05:50.230 --> 00:05:51.400
So we are able to do more

00:05:51.400 --> 00:05:53.275
the more that they are
comfortable with and can do.

00:05:58.050 --> 00:06:00.210
Sophia: So, we actually have
to have a close relationship

00:06:00.210 --> 00:06:02.410
with our operator when
we're learning this process

00:06:02.410 --> 00:06:04.535
because, you know, there's
only so much I can do.

00:06:09.680 --> 00:06:11.530
And so you have to get
together with our operator

00:06:11.530 --> 00:06:14.100
to give you more slack,
less slack, and tension.

00:06:14.100 --> 00:06:15.650
And help me spin and fly.

00:06:15.650 --> 00:06:17.250
So instead of just working with a partner,

00:06:17.250 --> 00:06:19.225
you're also working with another partner.

00:06:25.150 --> 00:06:27.760
Narrator: Along with aerial
work, the show's cast

00:06:27.760 --> 00:06:31.730
must develop other skills
that go beyond skating.

00:06:31.730 --> 00:06:33.220
Nelson: This is called a rollup.

00:06:33.220 --> 00:06:36.523
And it uses a lot of abs
and upper-body strength.

00:06:37.580 --> 00:06:39.240
It really works your back
and all these muscles

00:06:39.240 --> 00:06:41.320
that we never really
use as figure skaters.

00:06:41.320 --> 00:06:42.620
But we have to use it now.

00:06:44.500 --> 00:06:46.170
Narrator: Nelson's upper-body strength

00:06:46.170 --> 00:06:48.740
is put to the test
during the "Mary Poppins"

00:06:48.740 --> 00:06:50.083
segment of the show.

00:06:52.240 --> 00:06:54.110
Nelson: Being able to hold on for grip

00:06:54.110 --> 00:06:56.450
for so long up there, all
my weight's in my hands

00:06:56.450 --> 00:06:57.340
for the first time ever.

00:06:57.340 --> 00:06:59.260
You know, we're used to being on skates

00:06:59.260 --> 00:07:01.363
on our own body; it's all upper-body now.

00:07:02.640 --> 00:07:04.930
Narrator: The "Mary Poppins"
segment also combines

00:07:04.930 --> 00:07:08.596
feats of upper-body
strength with aerial work,

00:07:08.596 --> 00:07:11.793
which occurs when the
lampposts suddenly take flight.

00:07:13.030 --> 00:07:14.690
Nelson: We always have communication;

00:07:14.690 --> 00:07:15.970
it's the most important part.

00:07:15.970 --> 00:07:18.248
If something goes wrong,
we have a few little waves,

00:07:18.248 --> 00:07:19.600
thumbs down means down,

00:07:19.600 --> 00:07:21.730
you know, feet like this also means down,

00:07:21.730 --> 00:07:24.280
like an emergency exit, in
case something goes wrong.

00:07:24.280 --> 00:07:27.440
At first we started low, but
the higher we started going,

00:07:27.440 --> 00:07:29.053
it's definitely a little scary.

00:07:31.220 --> 00:07:33.560
Narrator: Not every
role in "Disney on Ice"

00:07:33.560 --> 00:07:37.223
requires quite the same
level of athletic ability.

00:07:38.960 --> 00:07:42.835
But each part contributes
to the overall experience.

00:07:46.980 --> 00:07:47.813
Bryan: Especially figure skaters,

00:07:47.813 --> 00:07:49.538
it's a very solitary sport.

00:07:49.538 --> 00:07:51.925
We don't grow up always working with,

00:07:51.925 --> 00:07:53.503
like, a large group together.

00:07:55.080 --> 00:07:56.280
Getting them to see the bigger picture,

00:07:56.280 --> 00:07:59.233
know that it's not yourself
out on the ice by yourself,

00:07:59.233 --> 00:08:00.470
you're a part of a huge unit

00:08:00.470 --> 00:08:02.300
that's performing in front
of thousands of people.

00:08:02.300 --> 00:08:03.870
So, it's kinda getting the mindset

00:08:03.870 --> 00:08:05.688
of your everyday figure skater into the

00:08:05.688 --> 00:08:07.180
mindset of a professional athlete.

00:08:08.240 --> 00:08:10.100
They've grown a really close bond,

00:08:10.100 --> 00:08:11.170
and they've become really good friends,

00:08:11.170 --> 00:08:12.010
and they support each other.

00:08:12.010 --> 00:08:14.660
It's a really good group of
people that we have here.

00:08:15.680 --> 00:08:17.599
Nelson: When I go out there
and I see the audience react

00:08:17.599 --> 00:08:20.714
so well to us, and all
these families come together

00:08:20.714 --> 00:08:22.100
and share such a beautiful moment,

00:08:22.100 --> 00:08:23.400
it gives me so much joy.

00:08:23.400 --> 00:08:26.750
Makes me miss my family,
but they're proud of me.

00:08:26.750 --> 00:08:28.258
I know they're proud of me.

